@title Audit The Route, Not Only The Repairs
@keywords route, causal-model, warrants, progress-metric, structural-difficulty
@audience operators and agents running long repair campaigns
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status draft 2026-10-11 (claude-11)
@why translation/revise-the-source-under-pressure
@see-also parsing/run-the-reference-before-modelling-it futon-theory/stop-the-line
@violation-signature every repair carries a warrant while the approach carries none; a progress metric defined along the chosen route; rigour rising as pace stays flat.
! conclusion: Hold the route to the same evidential standard as each repair, by stating what would show the whole approach wrong and checking it on a schedule.

  + context: A long campaign makes many repairs toward a fixed target.

  + IF:
    Repairs are audited carefully (each with a mechanism, a warrant, a test).

  + HOWEVER:
    Audit at the level of repairs makes the campaign look well run whatever its direction, and its progress metric counts steps along the route it already chose, so it cannot show the route is wrong.

  + THEN:
    Write down, for the route itself, a falsifier: what result or pace would show another approach is better. Name the alternatives and the cheapest test that would decide between them. Revisit at fixed intervals and whenever pace stalls, and treat a stall as possibly structural rather than a queue of local fixes.

  + BECAUSE:
    A route is a hypothesis too; careful repairs only tell you how well the route is being followed.

  + evidence: The private port above. Its maintenance layer required each of 25 repair mechanisms to carry causal arrows with warrants and falsifiable checks, while a review of the wiring work noted that it did not yet validate the port. Progress was counted as declarations compiled, a measure defined along the chosen route, and it rose slowly and then regressed. Alternative routes were first written down and compared ten days in.